<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Find US distributors by starting with your buyers: ask who they buy from today, then look at which distributors carry the products yours sit next to. Vet them on fit, not size. The right distributor already sells to your customers, has a team that can explain your product, and will commit to specific actions in the first months. A large distributor that adds you to a catalog and does nothing else is not a channel.</p>

<h2 id="where-to-look">Where to look</h2>
<p>The most reliable sources are the ones closest to the buyer.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Your target customers.</strong> Ask a handful of US buyers where they purchase products like yours and which suppliers they trust. Their answers narrow the field faster than any directory.</li>
  <li><strong>Adjacent and competing products.</strong> Distributors that carry complementary products already reach your buyers. Competitors' websites often list their distribution partners openly.</li>
  <li><strong>Resellers.</strong> If your category sells through resellers, ask which distributors they prefer to buy from and why. Their view of service, credit and logistics is valuable.</li>
  <li><strong>Trade shows and industry associations.</strong> Distributor teams attend the events your buyers attend. Associations publish member lists that include distributors.</li>
  <li><strong>Government and trade programs.</strong> Many countries run export promotion offices in the US that can make introductions. SelectUSA, the US government program for foreign companies setting up in the US, can point you to state economic development offices, which often know the distributors in their region.</li>
</ul>
<p>Build a long list, then cut it with a short set of questions before any meeting.</p>

<h2 id="first-filter">The first filter</h2>
<p>Before you ask for a meeting, answer these from public information and a short call:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Do they sell to the customers you need, in the regions you need?</li>
  <li>Do they carry products that complement yours rather than compete head on?</li>
  <li>Do they have the technical capability your product needs, such as pre-sales engineers, demo facilities or certified support staff?</li>
  <li>Do they work with vendors of your size, or only with large brands?</li>
  <li>Can they import, if you need them to, or do they expect you to deliver into the US?</li>
</ol>
<p>A distributor that fails on the first two will not sell your product, however strong its brand.</p>

<h2 id="the-vetting-conversation">The vetting conversation</h2>
<p>Once a distributor is interested, treat the next conversations as mutual due diligence. Ask questions that reveal how they will actually work with you:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Who would manage your line, and how many other vendors do they handle?</li>
  <li>How do they launch a new vendor: training, a campaign to their resellers, joint calls, a launch event?</li>
  <li>What do they expect from you in marketing support, training and stock commitments?</li>
  <li>How do they report sales and inventory to vendors, and how often?</li>
  <li>What are their payment terms to you, and their credit terms to customers?</li>
  <li>How do they handle returns, warranty claims and technical escalations?</li>
  <li>What would a successful first period look like to them, in specific terms?</li>
</ul>
<p>Vague answers to these questions are a warning. A distributor that will work hard for you can describe exactly how.</p>

<h2 id="references">Check references properly</h2>
<p>Ask for two or three vendors of similar size who joined in the last couple of years, and speak to them directly. Ask what the distributor did in the first months, how responsive the product manager was, and whether sales met what was discussed at signing. Speak to one or two resellers who buy from the distributor too. Resellers will tell you about order accuracy, delivery times and support, which vendors rarely see.</p>
<p>Basic financial checks matter as well. A distributor that holds your stock and collects from customers is extending credit on your behalf. Business credit reports and trade references help you judge whether it pays on time.</p>

<h2 id="start-small">Start with a defined commitment</h2>
<p>Rather than signing a long exclusive agreement at the start, agree on a first period with specific commitments on both sides: the training they will run, the resellers or accounts they will introduce you to, the marketing you will fund, and the stock or sales levels you both expect. Review it at the end of that period. Exclusivity, if you grant it at all, works better as something a distributor earns through results than something it receives on signing.</p>

<h2 id="be-vetted-too">Expect to be vetted too</h2>
<p>Good distributors are selective, because every vendor they add takes their team's time. Expect them to ask about your US pricing, your stock and delivery plans, the marketing you will fund, the training you will provide and how quickly you answer technical questions. Prepare clear answers before the first meeting. A vendor that arrives organized is easier to say yes to.</p>

<h2 id="red-flags">Red flags</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Large upfront fees for listing or marketing with no clear plan behind them.</li>
  <li>A demand for national exclusivity before any sales.</li>
  <li>No named person to manage your line.</li>
  <li>Reluctance to share sales or inventory reports.</li>
  <li>References that are all long-standing, very large vendors with different needs from yours.</li>
</ul>
<p>Choosing a distributor is one of the hardest decisions to reverse in a new market. Taking a few extra weeks to vet the shortlist costs far less than unwinding the wrong agreement.</p>
